1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,949
At dusk, the lamplighter starts the round.

2
00:00:13,390 --> 00:00:15,968
On the corner stands a bank, and in its

3
00:00:16,026 --> 00:00:18,638
wall a small screen glows all night.

4
00:00:19,334 --> 00:00:20,588
It never closes.

5
00:00:21,029 --> 00:00:22,980
It never needs a lunch break.

6
00:00:23,677 --> 00:00:26,753
Inside, behind the counter, there were

7
00:00:26,811 --> 00:00:28,866
once people at every window.

8
00:00:29,563 --> 00:00:32,245
Tonight, only a few of those windows are

9
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:32,848
lit.

10
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,273
For most of the twentieth century, banking

11
00:00:55,343 --> 00:00:57,072
meant a person at a window.

12
00:00:57,583 --> 00:00:59,836
The teller counted your cash, checked your

13
00:00:59,894 --> 00:01:02,297
signature, and wrote the deposit in your

14
00:01:02,355 --> 00:01:03,098
passbook.

15
00:01:03,609 --> 00:01:07,011
It was careful, repetitive work, and after

16
00:01:07,069 --> 00:01:09,646
the Second World War there was more and

17
00:01:09,762 --> 00:01:10,482
more of it.

18
00:01:10,993 --> 00:01:12,757
In 1950, the U.S.

19
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,102
census counted about sixty-five thousand

20
00:01:15,161 --> 00:01:16,031
bank tellers.

21
00:01:16,472 --> 00:01:20,199
By 1970, on a newer census basis, about

22
00:01:20,257 --> 00:01:22,428
two hundred fifty-three thousand.

23
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:25,993
That growth came before cash machines, in

24
00:01:26,051 --> 00:01:28,001
the long postwar boom.

25
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,076
Then came a machine built for the most

26
00:01:32,145 --> 00:01:33,794
repetitive part of the job.

27
00:01:34,398 --> 00:01:36,615
Cash machines were first installed in the

28
00:01:36,685 --> 00:01:38,914
United States in the 1970s.

29
00:01:39,518 --> 00:01:42,037
A machine could hand out cash, take a

30
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:45,323
deposit, and report a balance, at any

31
00:01:45,404 --> 00:01:48,016
hour, without a person at the window.

32
00:01:48,829 --> 00:01:51,755
From the mid-1990s, banks rolled them out

33
00:01:51,824 --> 00:01:52,486
fast.

34
00:01:52,927 --> 00:01:56,526
By 2015, economist James Bessen counted

35
00:01:56,585 --> 00:01:58,721
more than four hundred thousand of them in

36
00:01:58,767 --> 00:02:00,265
the United States alone.

37
00:02:00,961 --> 00:02:03,342
You might expect the tellers to vanish.

38
00:02:03,945 --> 00:02:05,095
They did not.

39
00:02:06,883 --> 00:02:07,638
Why not?

40
00:02:07,812 --> 00:02:09,727
Because the machine made each branch

41
00:02:09,809 --> 00:02:10,888
cheaper to run.

42
00:02:11,399 --> 00:02:14,697
Between 1988 and 2004, the number of

43
00:02:14,766 --> 00:02:17,007
tellers needed to operate an average urban

44
00:02:17,053 --> 00:02:19,399
branch fell from twenty to thirteen.

45
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:21,848
So banks did something unexpected.

46
00:02:22,092 --> 00:02:23,637
They opened more branches.

47
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:26,644
In urban areas, Bessen reports,

48
00:02:26,818 --> 00:02:28,443
forty-three percent more.

49
00:02:29,047 --> 00:02:31,868
Fewer tellers per branch, but many more

50
00:02:31,903 --> 00:02:32,611
branches.

51
00:02:33,424 --> 00:02:36,593
Bessen puts the logic simply: the ATM let

52
00:02:36,663 --> 00:02:39,240
banks run branch offices at lower cost,

53
00:02:39,519 --> 00:02:41,621
and that prompted them to open many more

54
00:02:41,667 --> 00:02:42,132
of them.

55
00:02:42,828 --> 00:02:44,895
A machine that saved labor at each window

56
00:02:44,976 --> 00:02:47,240
created demand for more windows.

57
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:50,989
And the job itself changed.

58
00:02:51,372 --> 00:02:53,357
Counting cash mattered less.

59
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:56,237
Talking to customers mattered more.

60
00:02:56,678 --> 00:02:59,104
In Bessen's words, tellers became an

61
00:02:59,151 --> 00:03:01,206
important part of the relationship banking

62
00:03:01,275 --> 00:03:05,037
team: selling accounts, explaining loans,

63
00:03:05,362 --> 00:03:06,743
solving problems.

64
00:03:07,022 --> 00:03:09,112
In census and survey data,

65
00:03:09,356 --> 00:03:11,515
full-time-equivalent teller employment

66
00:03:11,724 --> 00:03:14,882
kept rising after 2000, by about two

67
00:03:14,975 --> 00:03:17,297
percent a year, faster than the labor

68
00:03:17,367 --> 00:03:18,481
force as a whole.

69
00:03:19,085 --> 00:03:21,697
Bessen's point is not that machines never

70
00:03:21,767 --> 00:03:23,021
cost jobs.

71
00:03:23,404 --> 00:03:25,935
It is that when a machine takes one task,

72
00:03:26,086 --> 00:03:28,489
the job can survive if people move to the

73
00:03:28,547 --> 00:03:29,847
tasks that are left.

74
00:03:30,451 --> 00:03:32,529
The machine did not end the job.

75
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:35,025
It changed what the job was for.

76
00:03:38,542 --> 00:03:40,725
The lamplighter crosses the square.

77
00:03:41,328 --> 00:03:43,534
Most of the bank's windows are dark now,

78
00:03:43,743 --> 00:03:46,344
but the screen by the door still glows.

79
00:03:46,948 --> 00:03:49,165
The work has moved again: from the

80
00:03:49,223 --> 00:03:53,403
counter, to the phone, to an app, and now

81
00:03:53,484 --> 00:03:56,398
to software that reads, scores, and

82
00:03:56,479 --> 00:03:57,350
answers.

83
00:04:06,842 --> 00:04:09,152
Today, the outlook for tellers is

84
00:04:09,210 --> 00:04:09,756
different.

85
00:04:10,197 --> 00:04:12,589
The Bureau of Labor Statistics puts their

86
00:04:12,647 --> 00:04:14,563
number at about three hundred thirty-nine

87
00:04:14,609 --> 00:04:16,850
thousand in 2025.

88
00:04:17,291 --> 00:04:19,520
It projects about two hundred ninety-five

89
00:04:19,555 --> 00:04:23,189
thousand by 2035, a decline of thirteen

90
00:04:23,282 --> 00:04:23,886
percent.

91
00:04:24,489 --> 00:04:27,531
The reasons it gives are familiar: online

92
00:04:27,578 --> 00:04:30,968
and mobile banking, enhanced ATMs, and

93
00:04:31,049 --> 00:04:32,361
video kiosks.

94
00:04:32,872 --> 00:04:35,542
That is a projection, not a count.

95
00:04:37,342 --> 00:04:39,385
The machines are also moving further up

96
00:04:39,432 --> 00:04:41,823
the counter, to the people who decide who

97
00:04:41,882 --> 00:04:42,671
gets credit.

98
00:04:43,368 --> 00:04:45,539
Loan officer jobs are projected to grow

99
00:04:45,620 --> 00:04:48,197
only about one percent, from two hundred

100
00:04:48,313 --> 00:04:50,345
eighty-three thousand to two hundred

101
00:04:50,415 --> 00:04:53,456
eighty-six thousand, as branches close and

102
00:04:53,538 --> 00:04:56,034
loan processing becomes more automated.

103
00:04:56,730 --> 00:04:59,935
Most firms, the BLS notes, already use

104
00:05:00,004 --> 00:05:01,583
underwriting software.

105
00:05:03,383 --> 00:05:05,519
Behind the loan officer sits the credit

106
00:05:05,577 --> 00:05:08,073
analyst, who studies the numbers behind a

107
00:05:08,131 --> 00:05:08,677
loan.

108
00:05:09,118 --> 00:05:11,684
In a 2025 article on artificial

109
00:05:11,754 --> 00:05:15,562
intelligence, BLS economists wrote that AI

110
00:05:15,632 --> 00:05:18,453
can synthesize large amounts of data and

111
00:05:18,546 --> 00:05:21,216
reach big-picture conclusions, and that

112
00:05:21,274 --> 00:05:23,747
these tasks are the essence of a credit

113
00:05:23,817 --> 00:05:24,397
rating.

114
00:05:25,094 --> 00:05:27,544
In that article's projection, an earlier

115
00:05:27,625 --> 00:05:31,979
one for 2023 to 2033, credit analyst jobs

116
00:05:32,025 --> 00:05:34,045
were set to decline three point nine

117
00:05:34,092 --> 00:05:34,718
percent.

118
00:05:35,229 --> 00:05:37,575
It belongs to a different projection cycle

119
00:05:37,621 --> 00:05:39,003
from the other numbers.

120
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:43,041
Does the software actually judge credit

121
00:05:43,099 --> 00:05:43,586
better?

122
00:05:44,283 --> 00:05:46,373
Economists at the Federal Reserve Bank of

123
00:05:46,419 --> 00:05:48,683
Philadelphia tested a machine-learning

124
00:05:48,730 --> 00:05:49,566
model on U.S.

125
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:50,947
credit bureau records.

126
00:05:51,388 --> 00:05:55,254
In every year from 2004 to 2019, it

127
00:05:55,312 --> 00:05:57,205
predicted defaults better than a

128
00:05:57,251 --> 00:05:59,074
traditional statistical model.

129
00:05:59,457 --> 00:06:01,779
It is a test of models, not a count of

130
00:06:01,849 --> 00:06:02,511
jobs.

131
00:06:02,836 --> 00:06:04,984
It shows the scoring is getting better.

132
00:06:05,309 --> 00:06:07,039
It does not show what happens to the

133
00:06:07,074 --> 00:06:08,246
people who did it.

134
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:12,444
Ask someone who has worked in banking, and

135
00:06:12,491 --> 00:06:13,791
the line looks like this.

136
00:06:14,522 --> 00:06:17,297
Machines took the routine: cash,

137
00:06:17,646 --> 00:06:19,677
statements, transfers.

138
00:06:20,223 --> 00:06:22,533
Models now score a loan in seconds.

139
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:26,040
But a model answers one question — how

140
00:06:26,121 --> 00:06:27,108
risky is this?

141
00:06:27,653 --> 00:06:30,103
People still answer another — should we

142
00:06:30,173 --> 00:06:32,205
lend, and on what terms?

143
00:06:32,901 --> 00:06:34,910
And sometimes the most important thing a

144
00:06:34,968 --> 00:06:37,522
teller does is notice that something is

145
00:06:37,592 --> 00:06:41,342
wrong: a customer, nervous, sending money

146
00:06:41,411 --> 00:06:42,352
to a stranger.

147
00:06:42,863 --> 00:06:45,254
The machine processes the request.

148
00:06:45,765 --> 00:06:47,774
A person asks why.

149
00:06:50,092 --> 00:06:52,251
So here is the comparison, on the same

150
00:06:52,275 --> 00:06:56,501
scale: one source, one period, 2025 to

151
00:06:56,570 --> 00:06:57,673
2035.

152
00:06:58,068 --> 00:07:00,704
Bank tellers: down about thirteen percent.

153
00:07:01,098 --> 00:07:03,374
Loan officers: up about one percent.

154
00:07:03,769 --> 00:07:06,160
All jobs together: up three point five

155
00:07:06,207 --> 00:07:06,717
percent.

156
00:07:07,263 --> 00:07:09,817
For tellers, the BLS points to online

157
00:07:09,864 --> 00:07:13,126
banking and machines, not to AI alone.

158
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,168
Not the same technology as the ATM.

159
00:07:16,482 --> 00:07:19,616
The same pattern: a repetitive human task,

160
00:07:19,663 --> 00:07:21,718
taken over by an automated system.

161
00:07:22,298 --> 00:07:24,492
The ATM took over the cash.

162
00:07:24,934 --> 00:07:26,686
Software now takes on more of the

163
00:07:26,733 --> 00:07:28,555
paperwork and the scoring.

164
00:07:29,066 --> 00:07:31,017
But the differences matter.

165
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:34,314
Back then, cheaper branches meant more

166
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:36,555
branches, and more tellers.

167
00:07:37,066 --> 00:07:39,724
Now the BLS expects fewer branches.

168
00:07:40,107 --> 00:07:41,559
And all three of these numbers are

169
00:07:41,605 --> 00:07:43,567
forecasts for one decade.

170
00:07:44,008 --> 00:07:46,783
The ATM story is history.

171
00:07:47,294 --> 00:07:49,825
We set them side by side for the pattern,

172
00:07:49,976 --> 00:07:51,578
not for the arithmetic.

173
00:07:53,383 --> 00:07:55,345
The teller's story is the more hopeful

174
00:07:55,415 --> 00:07:56,065
kind.

175
00:07:56,506 --> 00:07:58,747
A machine took over the most repetitive

176
00:07:58,793 --> 00:08:02,125
task, and for decades the job grew anyway,

177
00:08:02,334 --> 00:08:04,029
because people did more of what the

178
00:08:04,076 --> 00:08:08,267
machine could not: advice, trust, a face

179
00:08:08,313 --> 00:08:09,254
at the window.

180
00:08:09,765 --> 00:08:11,599
But that came with a condition.

181
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:14,548
Cheaper branches meant more branches.

182
00:08:14,989 --> 00:08:17,415
That condition may not hold now.

183
00:08:17,856 --> 00:08:20,364
With AI, it is an open question.

184
00:08:20,805 --> 00:08:22,942
It depends on whether banks create new

185
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,658
work faster than software takes the old

186
00:08:25,716 --> 00:08:26,599
work away.

187
00:08:27,202 --> 00:08:29,513
That is what we will be watching.

188
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:35,450
Back on the corner, the lamplighter

189
00:08:35,543 --> 00:08:37,029
reaches the last post.

190
00:08:37,471 --> 00:08:40,385
The glass opens, the flame takes.

191
00:08:41,081 --> 00:08:43,206
Across the street, the little screen

192
00:08:43,322 --> 00:08:45,261
glows, patient as ever.

193
00:08:45,865 --> 00:08:48,245
Every era has its last lamplighters.

194
00:08:48,686 --> 00:08:50,996
Some of them stood behind a bank window,

195
00:08:51,437 --> 00:08:53,376
and many still do.

